The derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given by

$$
\dfrac{\textrm{d}}{\textrm{d}x}(\arsinh x) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+1}}.
$$

Therefore, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}\int \frac{-3}{\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\,\text{d}x=-3\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\,\text{d}x=-3\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x+C.\end{aligned}
$$

